                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Input I. x0 = vmirror(I) (rows reversed order: mirror along vertical means reverse each row? Wait let's check dsl.\n\nvmirror: if isinstance(piece, tuple): return tuple(row[::-1] for row in piece). So vmirror reverses each row (left-right). hmirror reverses row order (top-bottom).\n\nLet's compute: I is 3x2.\nx0 = vmirror(I): reverse each row -> same since symmetric? I rows [3,0] reversed = [0,3]. So x0 = [[0,3],[0,0],[0,3]].\nx1 = hconcat(x0, I): each row x0 row + I row -> [[0,3,3,0],[0,0,0,0],[0,3,3,0]] (3x4).\nx2 = hmirror(x1) = reverse row order: [[0,3,3,0],[0,0,0,0],[0,3,3,0]] \u2014 symmetric so same.\nx3 = vconcat(x1,x2) = 6x4: x1 rows then x2 rows.\nx4 = vconcat(x3,x1) = 9x4: x3 then x1.\nx5 = hmirror(x4) = reverse rows of x4.\n\nx4 rows: x1 (3 rows), x2(3 rows), x1(3 rows). Reversing order gives x1 reversed, x2 reversed, x1 reversed... hmm x5 output given:\nrows: [0,3,3,0],[0,0,0,0],[0,3,3,0],[0,3,3,0],[0,0,0,0],[0,3,3,0],[0,3,3,0],[0,0,0,0],[0,3,3,0] which is same.\n\nSo overall output is 3h x 4w = 9x4, all built from mirrored concatenations. Simpler description: output = vconcat of [X, hmirror(X), X] reversed? Actually final result: take a = hconcat(vmirror(I), I) (height h, width 2w). Then output is 3h x 2w where rows block: block a, then hmirror(a), then a? then reverse order... Let's just compute directly: x5 = hmirror(x4) = reverse row order of x4. x4 = x1, x2, x1 where x2 = hmirror(x1). Reverse: reverse(x1), reverse(x2), reverse(x1) = hmirror(x1), hmirror(x2), hmirror(x1). hmirror(x2)= hmirror(hmirror(x1)) = x1. So x5 = x2, x1, x2 = hmirror(x1), x1, hmirror(x1).\n\nSince example symmetric it's same. So output = vertical stacking of hmirror(a), a, hmirror(a) where a = hconcat(vmirror(I), I) = each row: reverse(I' row) + row.\n\nSimpler: a is 2w wide, each row = reversed row + row. Output = 3 blocks stacked.\n\nThe verifier is the ground truth. So I just need to provide input grids; environment computes outputs. I need to pick good inputs (diverse, not all symmetric) so student can infer.\n\nImportant: The example is symmetric, hiding some details. But verifier is exact; I don't need to interpret, just choose inputs. Better to choose asymmetric inputs so the rule is revealed properly. Since student learns from pairs, providing non-symmetric inputs is fine and more informative.\n\nLet me pick a few inputs of various sizes.\n\nInput 1: 2x3 asymmetric, e.g. [[1,2,3],[4,5,6]].\nInput 2: 1x2 [[7,8]].\nInput 3: 3x1 [[9],[0],[5]]? Note \"four black box and two colored box\" - whatever.\n\nLet's just give a few diverse rectangles. Need rectangular matrices, integers 0-9.\n\nLet's produce like 3-4 pairs.\n\nInput A: [[1,2,3],[4,5,6]] (2x3).\nInput B: [[7,0],[0,8]] (2x2).\nInput C: [[1,2,3,4]] (1x4).\nInput D: [[5],[6],[7]] (3x1).\n\nLet's submit.",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
